<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Titus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">39–81</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Titus was Roman emperor from 79 to 81 CE, the elder son of Vespasian and the second emperor of the Flavian dynasty. In 70 he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> after a long siege, destroying the city and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a>, an event commemorated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arch-of-titus">Arch of Titus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. As emperor he dealt with the eruption of Vesuvius in 79 and opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colosseum">Colosseum</a> in 80.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/titus.jpg" alt="Titus" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titus_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek_IN3159.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Titus was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the year 39, probably on 30 December, the eldest son of Vespasian and Domitilla the Elder. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a> was born in 51. The Flavian family had risen from obscurity to prominence in four generations under the Julio-Claudian emperors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> records that Titus was brought up at the imperial court with Britannicus, the son of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>. He served as a military tribune in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a> from about 57 to 59, and also in Britannia, and returned to Rome about 63 to practise law and become a quaestor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> revolted in 66, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> sent Vespasian to put down the rebellion, and Titus joined him at Ptolemais with the Fifteenth Legion. By 68 the coast and north of Judaea had been subdued, with victories at Taricheae and Gamala, where Titus distinguished himself. The campaign stopped when news came of Nero's death, and after Vespasian was declared emperor in 69, Titus was left to end the rebellion.</p><p>In 70 he besieged and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, destroying the city and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a>. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 71 and rode in a triumph with Vespasian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a>, in which treasures taken from the Temple were paraded, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arch-of-titus">Arch of Titus</a> at the Forum commemorates the victory. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, who wrote the main account of the war, joined his entourage and took the name Flavius, and the neutrality of his work has been questioned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Titus succeeded Vespasian on his death in the summer of 79, the first Roman emperor to follow his own father. He ended the treason trials that had long troubled the principate. A few months into his reign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-vesuvius">Mount Vesuvius</a> erupted and buried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompeii">Pompeii</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herculaneum">Herculaneum</a>. He appointed two ex-consuls to organise relief and gave large sums from the imperial treasury.</p><p>A fire in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 80 burned for three days and three nights and destroyed many public buildings, and he again paid for repairs. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colosseum">Colosseum</a>, begun in 70 under Vespasian, was completed in 80, and its inaugural games lasted a hundred days. Titus died of a fever on 13 September 81 after about two years in office, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a> succeeded him and deified him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a> say he died naturally, though Philostratus reports a poisoning. Ancient historians judged him a good emperor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Titus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The building had reached the third storey by Vespasian's death in 79; his son <b>Titus</b> finished the top level in 80, and the inaugural games were held in 80 or 81.</blockquote><a href="/work/colosseum">Colosseum · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>During the First Jewish–Roman War, Roman legions under <b>Titus</b> retook Jerusalem and destroyed much of the city and the Second Temple.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-30">30 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Vespasian subdued Galilee in 67 and <b>Titus</b> besieged Jerusalem in 70, destroying the city and the Second Temple that summer.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-judea">Provisional Government of Judea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Aquae Cutiliae, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=66" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Titus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=66">What was here in 66 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;My friends, I have lost a day.&#8221;</p><footer>Suetonius, Life of Titus, 8 &#183; tr. Alexander Thomson, 1796, rev. T. Forester &#183; on a day when he had done no one a kindness; Suetonius' account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/6400/pg6400-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
